`#test(should_panic: "substring")` — Panic Message Matching
============================================================

Per reference §"`should_panic`":

```sysl
#test(should_panic: "out of range")
test_bounds() -> unit
    // substring match: panic message must contain "out of range"
    panic("index 42 is out of range")
```

A `should_panic: "<text>"` test **passes** iff the body panics AND
the panic's message contains `<text>` as a substring. A panic with a
non-matching message fails the test.

This file pins:

- `should_panic: "..."` parses and runs.
- Substring matching works for ASCII text in any position (prefix,
  middle, suffix) of the panic message.
- Whole-message match (the entire panic message equals the substring)
  works.
- The substring can include punctuation and Unicode.
- `assert(false, "msg")` panics with `msg`, so the substring match
  applies to assertion failures too.
- A failing-assert with a matching substring is equivalent to a
  matching `panic`.

Note: testing the *negative* case (panic message doesn't contain the
substring → test fails) requires the runner to expose "expected
failure" semantics — not available in this corpus. The pin here is
that matching panic messages satisfy the expectation.

File-local prefix `Tspm` (Test-Should-Panic-Message).

    module sysl.tests.lang_features


### Substring matches as suffix

The reference's worked example: panic msg ends with the matched text.

    #test(should_panic: "out of range")
    tspm_suffix() -> unit
        panic("index 42 is out of range")

### Substring matches as prefix

Match at the start of the panic message.

    #test(should_panic: "bad input")
    tspm_prefix() -> unit
        panic("bad input received from caller")

### Substring matches in the middle

Match somewhere mid-message.

    #test(should_panic: "must be positive")
    tspm_middle() -> unit
        panic("argument n must be positive, got -3")

### Whole panic message equals the substring

The substring covers the entire message — exact-match case.

    #test(should_panic: "exact")
    tspm_whole() -> unit
        panic("exact")

### Substring with punctuation

The substring isn't restricted to alphanumerics.

    #test(should_panic: "got (-1)")
    tspm_punct() -> unit
        panic("unexpected return: got (-1) from helper")

### Substring with Unicode characters

UTF-8 substrings in panic messages match byte-for-byte.

    #test(should_panic: "ñ é")
    tspm_unicode() -> unit
        panic("Spanish accent test — ñ é — both present")

### `assert(false, msg)` is equivalent to `panic(msg)`

A failing assert panics with the message — should_panic substring
match catches the assertion failure the same way.

    #test(should_panic: "invariant")
    tspm_assert() -> unit
        assert(false, "loop invariant violated")
